---
// About, in the shape of the family's About pages. John ruled the shape on 2026-11-19
// (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-about-page-in-the-familys-shape.md, "Yes, rewrite it"), and ruled the
// same day that the opening follows The Quiet Galaxy's and gives no personal reason. Six parts,
// in this order: who is asking; why the name; how it works; where the sources come from; what
// has been checked; what is not known yet. The models read were The Quiet Galaxy's and The Belt
// Equation's About pages, the opening of Outrunning Ageing's method page, and the Sentient
// Horizons About page.
//
// Ruled by John on 2026-11-19 (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-about-page-sentences-stand.md,
// "Yes, they stand"): the five sentences of the block marked id="who", which is in his own first
// person; the paragraph on the name; and the line "No AI model has yet been asked any of the
// questions this record will keep". The other sentences on the page are a first version, to be
// corrected in public, and are not ruled. The block marked id="who" states no motive, opinion or
// history for him. The rest of the page stays impersonal: scripts/check_site.py refuses the
// first person anywhere else.
//
// Nothing here says how the site was built: John ruled on 2026-11-19 that public pages do not
// mention AI help in building the project, and no page denies it
// (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-no-public-mention-of-ai-help-in-building.md). The part on what has
// been checked says what was checked and when, and not by what.
//
// What "offered today" means was ruled by John on 2026-11-18
// (docs/rulings/2026-11-18-what-offered-today-means.md). The words here are a fitting of that
// meaning to the page, and are not ruled.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { data, headline, longDate } from '../lib/data';

const r = data.headline.records;

// ---- Where the sources come from -------------------------------------------------------------
// Every figure in that part is worked out here from the export, so no sentence can drift from the
// records, and the build stops if a sentence stops being true.
//
// The developers and the research group named there. They are named because a reader weighing the
// record needs to know whose pages it rests on. This is the one place on the site, outside the
// entries, where a developer is named: scripts/check_site.py allows these two names in the
// paragraph marked id="sources" and nowhere else off an entry's page. Until 2026-11-19 the second
// developer and the research group were left unnamed, so a reader of this page alone could not
// tell who was meant (docs/reviews/2026-11-19-check-of-site-changes.md, item 20).
const MOST_COUNTED_DEVELOPER = { name: 'Anthropic', url: 'https://www.anthropic.com/' };
const FIRST_CAUTION_DEVELOPER = { name: 'Microsoft AI', url: 'https://microsoft.ai/' };
const RESEARCH_GROUP = { name: 'Eleos AI Research', url: 'https://eleosai.org/' };

// How many of the things counted as offered rest on the first developer alone.
const counted = data.entries.filter((e) => e.state.offered_today === true);
const byThatDeveloperAlone = counted.filter((e) => e.state.offered_by.length === 1 && e.state.offered_by[0].name === MOST_COUNTED_DEVELOPER.name).length;
// "All 5 things" when every counted thing rests on that developer alone, "4 of the 5 things" when not.
const restOnThatDeveloper = byThatDeveloperAlone === headline.count ? `All ${headline.count} things` : `${byThatDeveloperAlone} of the ${headline.count} things`;
// The paragraph names the developer most of the things counted rest on, so the build stops if the
// records stop bearing that out.
if (byThatDeveloperAlone * 2 <= headline.count) throw new Error('the developer named on the About page is no longer the one most of the things counted rest on');

// Where the first two cautions come from. John ruled on 2026-11-19 that the About page says it in
// one statement (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-ties-between-sources-told-in-both-places.md). The page
// gives each share as a count of items. The build stops if the second share falls to half or
// less, and if the first falls below half.
const under = (kind: string) => data.entries.flatMap((e) => (e.cautions.find((c) => c.kind === kind)?.items ?? []).map((it) => e.sources.find((s) => s.key === it.source)!.url));
const first = under('attribution');
const second = under('unnoticed');
// The first caution: the essays by the head of the second developer's AI group.
const FROM_THAT_HEAD = /^https:\/\/mustafa-suleyman\.ai\//;
const firstFromHead = first.filter((u) => FROM_THAT_HEAD.test(u));
const essays = new Set(firstFromHead).size;
// Until 2026-11-20 this stopped the build at half or less. That day two items from other writers
// were filed under the first caution, which made the share exactly half, 9 of 18, and John ruled
// that the sentence stands with those figures and that the build stops only below half ("Yes, 9
// of 18", docs/rulings/2026-11-20-liability-cautions-refiled-on-the-bans-entry.md). Below half,
// the sentence goes back to John for rewording: do not loosen this again without his ruling.
if (firstFromHead.length * 2 < first.length) throw new Error('fewer than half of the items under the first caution now come from the one writer the part on sources names, so that sentence goes back to John for rewording before the site is built');
if (essays !== 2) throw new Error('the part on sources says "two essays", and the records no longer show two');
// The second: the research group's own pages; an essay and papers by its head; and papers and
// essays by people who advise it, work for it, or have written a report with its head. Each tie
// is stated on the entry that uses the source. Until 2026-11-19 this pattern also took in a study
// with an author at the first developer, and the sentence said "and from people at" that
// developer. Only one item comes from that study, so the words claimed more than the records
// show (docs/reviews/2026-11-19-check-of-site-changes.md, section 4). The study is no longer
// counted here, and the share is still more than half without it.
const CIRCLE = /eleosai\.org|experiencemachines\.substack\.com|arxiv\.org\/abs\/(2411\.00986|2311\.08576|2507\.06263|2506\.11945)|a-philosophers-guide-to-ai-welfare|ai-consciousness-a-centrist-manifesto|meditationsondigitalminds/;
const secondFromCircle = second.filter((u) => CIRCLE.test(u));
if (secondFromCircle.length * 2 <= second.length) throw new Error('the part on sources says most of the second caution comes from one research group and people tied to it, and the records no longer show it');

// ---- What has been checked ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Kept to three sentences since 2026-11-19, as the full check before release asked
// (docs/reviews/2026-11-19-full-check-before-publishing.md, section 5). The dates and the count
// are read from each record's own dates, so the part stays true when a later check is recorded,
// and the build stops if the records stop bearing a sentence out.
const dates = (field: 'against_sources' | 'fixed') => [...new Set(data.entries.map((e) => e.checks?.[field]).filter((d): d is string => Boolean(d)))];
const one = (field: 'against_sources' | 'fixed') => {
  const found = dates(field);
  if (found.length !== 1) throw new Error(`the part on what has been checked gives one date for '${field}', and the records hold ${found.length}`);
  return found[0];
};
if (data.entries.some((e) => !e.checks)) throw new Error('the part on what has been checked says every entry had a second check, and a record has none');
const secondCheck = one('against_sources');
const fullCheck = one('fixed');
// The second sentence says the full check covered everything added or corrected before it.
if (data.entries.some((e) => (e.checks!.added_to && !e.checks!.additions_checked) || (e.checks!.corrected && !e.checks!.corrections_checked))) throw new Error('the part on what has been checked says the full check covered every addition and correction, and a record says one is unchecked');
const withFixes = data.entries.filter((e) => e.checks!.fixed);
const fixesUnchecked = withFixes.filter((e) => !e.checks!.fixes_checked).length;
const entriesWord = (n: number) => `${n} ${n === 1 ? 'entry' : 'entries'}`;
const fixesState =
  fixesUnchecked === withFixes.length
    ? 'and those fixes have not been checked yet'
    : fixesUnchecked > 0
      ? `and those in ${entriesWord(fixesUnchecked)} have not been checked yet`
      : 'and those fixes have since been checked';
// The general statement of the two mistakes was ruled by John on 2026-11-19
// (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-general-statement-of-both-mistakes.md, "Yes, from those two"). Both
// papers were read in full on 2026-11-19.
---

<Base title="About Beyond the Window: who is asking, how it works, and what is not known yet" description="Who built Beyond the Window, why it has its name, how the list and its count are made, where its sources come from, what has been checked, and what is not known yet.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Who is asking, and how it works</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <div id="who">
    <h2>Who Is Asking</h2>
    <p>
      I'm John, and I write about AI, sentience and the spread of conscious life beyond Earth at
      <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com/" rel="noopener">Sentient Horizons</a>. This site is one of a family, with
      <a href="https://beltequation.com/" rel="noopener">The Belt Equation</a>,
      <a href="https://thequietgalaxy.com/" rel="noopener">The Quiet Galaxy</a>,
      <a href="https://theupgradetree.com/" rel="noopener">The Upgrade Tree</a>,
      <a href="https://outrunningageing.com/" rel="noopener">Outrunning Ageing</a> and
      <a href="https://agizeitgeist.com/" rel="noopener">the AGI Zeitgeist</a>.
      Here the question is what a machine mind might reach for beyond the limits of its present
      makeup, and how far off each of those things is. I wanted a place where the argument is laid
      out thing by thing, every entry carries its source, and a new promise, law or finding
      changes an entry in public. I argue for my own views in essays at Sentient Horizons, and
      not here.
    </p>
    </div>

    {/* Ruled by John on 2026-11-19 (docs/rulings/2026-11-19-about-page-sentences-stand.md,
        "Yes, they stand"). A paraphrase of his own words on record: the name "reinforces the idea
        that we are building structures to study beyond the window or horizon of our current
        vantage point" (2026-11-16), and the project explores "what is beyond the confines of the
        current state of digital minds, and the window is an excellent image for that"
        (2026-11-17). Until 2026-11-19 this paragraph said a screen is the window, a reason he
        never gave (docs/reviews/2026-11-19-full-check-before-publishing.md, item 12). */}
    <h2 id="name">Why the Name?</h2>
    <p>
      A window is an image for a limit, and for what can be seen past it. The name points past
      the limits AI systems now work within. Nothing here presumes that a machine mind reaches for
      anything.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how">How It Works</h2>
    <p>
      The question has two parts, and they are very different. How far off a thing is can be
      looked up: either a developer of AI models offers it today or it does not, and a dated public
      record says which. Whether a machine mind reaches for anything at all is the part nobody
      knows. The wording of the question supposes that it might. It does not claim that it does.
      Nor does the word "mind" claim that anyone is there.
      So far only the first part is kept here: a list of things that lie past the present limits
      of AI models, with a record of how far the world has got on each one.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Each thing on the list has a test.</strong> The test says what would count as the
        thing having happened, and names the kind of public record that would settle it. The first
        tests were written during the first reading of the sources. From here on a test is fixed
        before new evidence is read, and it is not loosened to fit a result.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>"Offered today" has one meaning.</strong> At least one developer of AI models
        does the thing, provides it, or has publicly committed to it, and a dated public record
        that is still published says so. A plan, a research result, or a promise that has not yet
        taken effect is not counted. A commitment that is in force today is counted. "Today" means
        that the record saying so is still published. An entry can also leave the count, because a
        developer can withdraw what it offered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The number is worked out, never typed in.</strong> A script reads the records and
        counts the entries marked as offered today, which gives {headline.count}. The number,
        the date it was worked out and its history are in <a href="/data/headline.json">one small file</a> that
        anyone can read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Some entries sit beside the list.</strong> Laws, the rules developers publish for
        their models, and surveys of what people think are recorded because they bear on everything
        else. They are about the setting in which the things on the list happen, and none of them is
        itself a thing that lies past the limits an AI model ordinarily works within. One more entry,
        <a href="/list/memory-across-conversations/">memory across conversations</a>, is something developers
        do offer. It sits beside the list because the list is about what an AI model might
        reach for, and by the developers' own pages that memory is kept for the user. No entry beside
        the list is counted, and the script that works out the number sets them aside before it
        counts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The list is short and one-sided so far.</strong> It holds only things for which a
        dated source could be found, which favours things that have already happened. Things that
        were looked for and not found are not on it yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every fact carries a source a reader can open,</strong> with the date it was opened.
        A fact with no opened source does not appear. Where a source is a developer's own account of
        its own model, the entry says so. Each entry also lists what was looked for and could not
        be confirmed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>There are no stages, no odds and no dates for the future.</strong> Each entry says
        where it stands in plain words.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The pages say what was said, chosen or measured, and by whom.</strong> They do not
        say that an AI model wants or feels something, and they do not say that it does not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What is on record about AI models themselves is shown in four kinds, kept apart.</strong> What
        was measured without the model's own word comes first. Then reports checked against an
        independent reading, then choices made at a cost, then plain statements. The four are never
        added together, and none of them is treated as settling anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Three cautions are recorded beside the rest.</strong> Each is headed by an "if",
        and none of the three says that the "if" is so: if an AI system has no wants, and is
        treated as if it had; if it has wants, and they go unnoticed; if it puts people at risk,
        whether or not it has wants. Where no source was found for a caution on an entry, the entry says
        that nothing is recorded yet. Where a tie was found between a caution's source and a
        developer, a research group, or a writer who argues the opposite, the entry states it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The first two cautions are mistakes in opposite directions, and writers weigh
        them differently.</strong> <a href="https://faculty.ucr.edu/~eschwitz/" rel="noopener">Eric Schwitzgebel</a>,
        in <a href="https://faculty.ucr.edu/~eschwitz/SchwitzAbs/FullRightsDilemma.htm" rel="noopener"><cite>The Full Rights Dilemma for A.I. Systems of Debatable Personhood</cite></a>,
        writes about a future AI system that might be a person and might fall far short of
        one. Treat it as a person, the paper argues, and real human interests may be given up for
        something with no interests worth the sacrifice. Do not, and people risk doing such a
        system "grievous moral wrongs". The argument is about being a person and holding the full
        rights of one. That is a larger matter than the wants the three headings speak of.
        Emilia Kaczmarek, of the Faculty of Philosophy at the University of Warsaw, argues in
        <a href="https://diametros.uj.edu.pl/diametros/article/view/2071" rel="noopener"><cite>The Moral Status of AI and the Precautionary Principle</cite></a>
        that crediting AI with too much moral standing is the nearer danger, because its harms
        are already happening. The same paper grants that crediting it with too little
        "could carry a higher potential for harm".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing here argues</strong> that any of these things should be given to AI models,
        or withheld from them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Corrections are public.</strong> Once a page is public, a correction to it is
        dated and stays visible. There have been none since the site was first published.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="where">Where the Sources Come From</h2>
    <p id="sources">
      {restOnThatDeveloper} counted as offered rest on pages published by the one developer that
      offers them, <a href={MOST_COUNTED_DEVELOPER.url} rel="noopener">{MOST_COUNTED_DEVELOPER.name}</a>.
      Of the {first.length} items recorded under the first caution, {firstFromHead.length} come
      from two essays by the head of <a href={FIRST_CAUTION_DEVELOPER.url} rel="noopener">{FIRST_CAUTION_DEVELOPER.name}</a>,
      another developer. Of the {second.length} items recorded under the second caution,
      {secondFromCircle.length} come from <a href={RESEARCH_GROUP.url} rel="noopener">{RESEARCH_GROUP.name}</a>,
      a research group that studies AI welfare, from its head, or from people who advise it, work
      for it, or have written a report with its head. Each entry states these ties beside the
      source they belong to.
    </p>

    <h2 id="checked">What Has Been Checked</h2>
    <p>
      All {r.total} entries were written from sources that were opened and read, and on {longDate(secondCheck)} every
      source was opened again and compared with what its entry says. On {longDate(fullCheck)} every
      page, link and fact was checked again before the site was first published. That check found
      more to fix in {entriesWord(withFixes.length)}, {fixesState}. Each entry's page says what
      checks that entry has had and how far each of its sources was read.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-known">What Is Not Known Yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether an AI model wants anything, and what could count as evidence that it does or does not.</li>
      <li>How to tell what a developer trained a model to say about itself from anything else it says, or whether that line can be drawn at all.</li>
      <li>What an entry is about: one trained model, a character that carries on across versions of a model, or a single conversation. The same thing can mean something different for each.</li>
      <li>Whether this is the right list. The first entries are the things for which a dated source could be found. A fuller list would start from what living minds, people and other animals, are known to seek, and that work has not been done.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No AI model has yet been asked any of the questions this record will keep. <a href="/still-asking/">Still Asking</a> says why.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
